{"id": "cma-135430", "status": "ok", "meta": "A dragon in waves and mist and a licking tiger, a pair of hanging scrolls by Fachang Muqi, about 1250 to 1279.", "about": "The dragon and tiger stand for yin and yang, opposite but complementary forces. The dragon fills a tiered background of waves and mist, with its bright head coming out of darker passages and a narrow, serpent-like body. The tiger is seen up close, which stresses its bulk, as it bends its head to lick a paw with its tail curled in front. In their original Chan Buddhist setting the pair of scrolls probably flanked a central image such as the bodhisattva Guanyin.", "museum_record": "https://clevelandart.org/art/1958.427", "inventory_number": "1958.427", "dimensions": "", "credit_line": "Purchase from the J. H. Wade Fund", "sources": ["https://clevelandart.org/art/1958.427", "https://openaccess-api.clevelandart.org/api/artworks/135430"], "check": "clean"}
