{"id": "cma-160300", "status": "ok", "meta": "Katsushika Ōi's hanging scroll of the bone-scraping of Guan Yu from Romance of the Three Kingdoms, made in the 1840s.", "about": "Katsushika Ōi used color to great effect in this gruesome version of an episode from the 14th-century Chinese novel Romance of the Three Kingdoms. The legendary general Guan Yu has a bone scraped to remove poison from an arrow wound. His attendants cower at the sight of his bloody arm while he stays unflinchingly focused on his game. The museum notes that Ōi was an outlier as a woman in her era.", "museum_record": "https://clevelandart.org/art/1998.178", "inventory_number": "1998.178", "dimensions": "Mounted: 206.7 x 73.1 cm (81 3/8 x 28 3/4 in.); Painting: 140.2 x 68.3 cm (55 3/16 x 26 7/8 in.)", "credit_line": "Kelvin Smith Fund", "sources": ["https://clevelandart.org/art/1998.178", "https://openaccess-api.clevelandart.org/api/artworks/160300"], "check": "clean"}
